  - type: ozel
    html: |-
      <div class="space-y-5">

                  <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
                    <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-sm font-bold shrink-0">1</div>
                    <div>
                      <h2 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">Retrieval Practice</h2>
                      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-2">Instead of re-reading material repeatedly, test yourself. Close the book and try to recall. This act of "retrieval" transfers knowledge to long-term memory and reduces exam anxiety — because the brain generates a signal of "I've already practised this."</p>
                      <p class="text-xs text-ink-light">How to apply: topic summaries, flashcards, explaining to someone else (the Feynman technique)</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>

                  <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
                    <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-sm font-bold shrink-0">2</div>
                    <div>
                      <h2 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">Spaced Repetition</h2>
                      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-2">All-night cramming sessions before an exam are both inefficient and increase anxiety. Spacing revision across multiple days increases long-term retention by up to three times. (Ebbinghaus, 1885; updated meta-analyses)</p>
                      <p class="text-xs text-ink-light">Tool recommendations: Anki, Quizlet or a simple revision timetable</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>

                  <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
                    <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-sm font-bold shrink-0">3</div>
                    <div>
                      <h2 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">Protect sleep quality</h2>
                      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-2">Sleep is the process through which newly learned information is consolidated in the brain. Staying up all night before an exam is equivalent to erasing a significant portion of what you learnt during the day. 7–9 hours of sleep both strengthens memory and regulates stress hormones.</p>
                      <p class="text-xs text-ink-light">Tip: Switch off screens 1 hour before bed; a light revision routine does not disrupt sleep quality.</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>

                  <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
                    <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-sm font-bold shrink-0">4</div>
                    <div>
                      <h2 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">4-7-8 breathing technique</h2>
                      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-2">When anxiety intensifies before or during study: inhale through your nose for 4 counts → hold for 7 counts → exhale through your mouth for 8 counts. Repeat this cycle 3–4 times. It activates the parasympathetic nervous system and calms the stress response within minutes.</p>
                      <p class="text-xs text-ink-light">Can also be applied silently inside the exam hall.</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>

                  <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
                    <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-sm font-bold shrink-0">5</div>
                    <div>
                      <h2 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">Cognitive reframing</h2>
                      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-2">Catastrophic thoughts such as "If I fail this exam, my life is over" multiply anxiety. Instead, question the thought: "This exam matters, but whatever the outcome, I can keep going." Stanford research shows that simply re-labelling anxiety as "excitement" improves performance. (Brooks, 2014)</p>
                      <p class="text-xs text-ink-light">Exercise: Write your worries on paper for 10 minutes before the exam — this "expressive writing" exercise reduces anxiety.</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>

                </div>
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: turuncu
    baslik: When should you seek professional support?
    metin: If exam anxiety is preventing you from sitting exams, causing you to restrict your social life, or leading to intense physical symptoms days in advance, working with a psychologist can fundamentally change the situation. Cognitive Behavioural Therapy has strong evidence for treating exam anxiety.
